# NEGATIVE CONTROL for `zone_invariant monotonic`. NOT a test of the module.
#
# The `.fixture` suffix keeps it out of any *.rule glob: it is REQUIRED to
# fail, and a runner that picked it up as an ordinary rule file would report a
# permanent red.
#
# HOW IT IS RIGGED
#   `monotonic` asserts that the field never DECREASED across the readings the
#   fan collected. zone.lookups is non-decreasing by construction (both addends
#   are lifetime fetch_add(+1) tallies), which is what case 5 of
#   shm-coherence-varidx.rule asserts.
#
#   This fixture points it at `smaps.pss` instead -- the answering worker's
#   proportional set size in KB, rendered by the generic probe from /proc.
#   It is PER-WORKER, and the fan deliberately spreads its 24 legs across four
#   of them, so consecutive readings routinely come from different processes
#   with different resident footprints. Measured directly against a live server
#   (four workers, sequential identical requests): 421, 502, 504, 504, 504,
#   504, 504, 504, 504, 420, 444, 444 -- the 504 -> 420 step at the worker
#   rotation is the decrease this control needs, and it is produced by the
#   fan's own worker spread rather than by anything the first request did.
#
#   `zone_invariant` takes any probe field path, not only a `zone.*` one --
#   assert.c's eval_zone_monotonic() compares the values it was handed and
#   never inspects the path. So a control is free to pick the field with the
#   most reliable failure, which is the whole job of a control.
#
# THREE FIELDS THAT WERE TRIED FIRST AND DO NOT WORK -- recorded so the next
# reader does not spend the same time rediscovering them.
#
#   zone.varidx_inflight. It is the other field that legitimately goes both up
#   and down, but only BETWEEN a variant-index write being launched and that
#   write completing -- one event-loop turn. The fan's legs are sequential and
#   each leg's probe read follows its completed response, so every reading
#   samples a settled 0. Run for real: 24 readings of 0, a constant never
#   decreases, THE CONTROL PASSED.
#
#   zone.slab_pages_free. It falls as the zone fills, but only once per NEW
#   cache entry, and all 24 legs send the identical request -- so the single
#   drop happens on leg 1, before reading 1, and readings 1..24 are constant.
#   Measured directly against a live server: constant at 2032 across six
#   identical requests, and constant at 12 with the zone shrunk to 64k. THE
#   CONTROL PASSED. There is no per-leg substitution in the rule language, so
#   no constant request can make this field fall repeatedly.
#
#   connections.free. The obvious "it must go up and down" field, and it does
#   not: each fan leg closes its connection before the next opens, so the
#   worker's free-slot count returns to the same number every time. Measured:
#   constant at 125 across twelve requests, and the control PASSED.
#
#   The lesson all three share: a control must fail because of something the FAN
#   itself does, not because of something the first request did.
#
# EXPECTED FAILURE MESSAGE (the driver greps for its stable prefix):
#   zone_invariant monotonic smaps.pss: reading N is X but reading M was Y
#
# The driver greps for `zone_invariant monotonic smaps.pss:` -- keep the
# field below in step with it.
name    NEGATIVE CONTROL: monotonic must reject a field that legitimately falls
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
probe   smaps.pss >= 0
fanout  64 2
zone_invariant monotonic smaps.pss
